<DocsLayout
  slug="android-signing"
  title="Android signing"
  description="Upload a keystore once, get signed .aab files from then on."
>
  <h2 id="what-you-need">What you need</h2>
  <p>
    A Java keystore (<code>.jks</code> or <code>.keystore</code>) along with its store password,
    key alias, and key password.
  </p>

  <h2 id="uploading-your-keystore">Uploading your keystore</h2>
  <p>
    From the project's Secrets screen, upload the keystore file and its credentials once —
    encrypted at rest the same way as your iOS secrets. See <a href="/docs/secret-vault"
      >Secret Vault</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="staging-vs-production">Staging vs. production</h2>
  <p>
    Staging Android builds are unsigned debug builds — no secrets requested at all, so you can
    test a project before ever touching the Vault. Production builds request your keystore and
    produce a signed <code>.aab</code>, ready to upload to Google Play, gated by plan — see <a
      href="/docs/plans">plans &amp; limits</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="if-you-lose-your-keystore">If you lose your keystore</h2>
  <p>
    A Google Play listing is tied to the keystore that signed its first release — losing it means
    you can't publish an update under that same listing ever again. pkgr.app stores your keystore
    encrypted for signing builds, but it isn't a backup service: keep your own secure copy too.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
